  "result": "I've added `click.KeyValue`. `@click.option('--set', type=click.KeyValue())` now turns `key=value` into a `(key, value)` tuple. I added tests for it, and the full test suite passes (1915 passed, 102 skipped).\n\n**How it parses** (`src/click/types.py`, right after the UUID type):\n- It splits on the first `=` only, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`.\n- An empty value is allowed: `a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`. Only the key has to be non-empty.\n- Input with no `=` (such as `ab` or an empty string), or with an empty key (`=b`, `=`), fails with `'ab' is not a valid key=value pair.` This is a normal usage error, so the program exits with code 2.\n- A value that's already a tuple is passed through unchanged, so a tuple can be used as a default.\n- With `multiple=True`, `dict(pairs)` gives you a dictionary of all the pairs.\n\n**Other changes:**\n- Exported `KeyValue` in `src/click/__init__.py`.\n- Added it to `docs/api.md` and `docs/parameter-types.md`.\n- Added an entry to `CHANGES.md` under a new \"Version 8.6.0 (Unreleased)\" section, because 8.5.0 is already released. The class docstring also says it was added in 8.6, so change both if your next version number is different.\n- Added the tests to `tests/test_types.py`: valid inputs, the error cases (checking the message), and the `multiple=True` case.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
